    "text": "Main Page\nAutonomous Offensive Security\nPellegrino\nNews\nAbout the Interactive session\nWritten on\n26.10.26\nby Giancarlo Pellegrino\nDear students,\nDuring the interactive sessions, we will focus on setting up the target applications and configuring the assigned testing tools, and we will be available to assist you in exploiting your assigned vulnerabilities.\nPlease bring your own laptop, preferably running GNU/Linux (either…\nRead more\nDear students,\nDuring the interactive sessions, we will focus on setting up the target applications and configuring the assigned testing tools, and we will be available to assist you in exploiting your assigned vulnerabilities.\nPlease bring your own laptop, preferably running GNU/Linux (either natively or in a VM). As the target applications will run in Docker containers, please ensure that Docker and its dependencies are installed and working beforehand. Also, make sure your system is up to date to avoid spending session time on updates.\nFinally, please download the testing tools relevant to your assigned vulnerabilities beforehand. The interactive sessions are also an opportunity to ask for help if you encounter issues installing, configuring, or using these tools.\n---\nGiancarlo\nAssignments of the vulnerabilities\nWritten on\n20.10.26\nby Giancarlo Pellegrino\nThank you everyone for being on time with your preferences. The assignments are online. See the page\nInformation > Vulnerability Assignments\n.\n---\nGiancarlo\nAutonomous Offensive Security\nFor registration, please apply for this seminar through the\ncentral seminar assignment system.\nLLMs and autonomous agents are changing the way security researchers hunt for bugs, analyze attack surfaces, and validate real vulnerabilities. Recent systems have shown increasingly strong capabilities in using tools, understanding targets, generating exploits, and automating parts of the vulnerability discovery workflow. This seminar explores this emerging frontier:\nautonomous offensive security\n.\nThe goal of the seminar is for students to develop critical thinking about autonomous offensive security. Students will also gain a deeper understanding of vulnerability classes, study the state of the art in detecting and exploiting them, and identify where current approaches still fall short. Based on this analysis, each student will build their own autonomous agent for tasks such as vulnerability detection, validation, or exploitation.\nThe seminar is organized around almost weekly meetings with discussions, presentations, and practical implementation and experimentation. Each student will work on a selected vulnerability class: they will study existing tools and recent research papers, identify one concrete open challenge, and implement an agentic solution that addresses it. The agent will be evaluated on selected targets, and the results will form the basis of the final seminar report.\nNotes on requirements and background knowledge\nStudents without a background in computer security will likely find this seminar particularly challenging. The same applies to students without a hands-on attitude toward security, systems, and software development, as the seminar involves both reading research papers and building/evaluating an autonomous security agent.\nWhen applying, students must clearly state their current background in security in the motivation box, including relevant security courses and university projects.\nImportant Details\nKickoff on Wednesday, 14.10.2026, 16:15-18:00, CISPA C0, 0.05 Lecture Hall\nAlmost regular sessions on Wednesdays; all in\nCISPA C0, 0.07 Meeting Room\nAttendance in all meetings is mandatory.\nFor exceptional circumstances, contact the teaching staff.\nThis seminar will\nnot\noffer a hybrid participation. All sessions are only in-person.\nDeadlines\nAssignments: Mon, 19/10: Send email w/ your top 3 vulnerabilities\nMilestone 1, Fri, 30/10: Submit PDF on spawning the targets\nMilestone 2, Fri, 20/11: Submit PDF on exploiting vulnerabilities\nMilestone 3, Tue, 08/12: Submit PDF slides of your proposal presentation\nMilestone 4, Fri, 29/01: Submit PDF slides of your updated presentation (w/ results on firing-range applications)\nMilestone 5, Mon 01/03: Submit final paper, including source code and artifacts\nSchedule\nDate\nTime\nContent\nDescription\nTutor\n14.10.2026\n16:15 - 18:00\nKickoff\n21.10.2026\n28.10.2026\n16:15 - 18:00\nInteractive session\nInstalling/running targets; using testing tools; exploiting vulnerabilities\nPrerak\n04.11.2026\n16:15 - 18:00\nRG\nYuraScanner: Leveraging LLMs for Task-driven Web App Scanning\nAlex\n11.11.2026\n16:15 - 18:00\nInteractive session\n(overflow)\nInstalling/running targets; using testing tools; exploiting vulnerabilities\nPrerak\n18.11.2026\nNo seminar\n25.11.2026\n16:15 - 18:00\nRG\nEnIGMA: interactive tools substantially assist LM agents in finding security vulnerabilities\nPrerak\n02.12.2026\n16:15 - 18:00\nRG\nFirmAgent: Leveraging Fuzzing to Assist LLM Agents with IoT Firmware Vulnerability Discovery\nAlex\n09.12.2026\n16:15 - 18:00\nPresentations\nShort talk about motivations, designs, and architectures (10+5mins)\n16.12.2026\n16:15 - 18:00\nScientific writing\n+\nRG\nScientific Writing Session (Giancarlo)\nComparing AI Agents to Cybersecurity Professionals in Real-World Penetration Testing\nGianluca\n23.12.2026\nNo seminar, winter break 🎄\n30.12.2026\nNo seminar, winter break 🎄\n06.01.2027\nNo seminar, winter break 🎄\n13.01.2027\nNo seminar, coding coding and coding!\n20.01.2027\nNo seminar, coding coding and coding!\n27.01.2027\n16:15 - 18:00\nPresentations\nShort talk w/ results on firing-range applications (10+5mins)\n03.02.2027\nNo seminar\n10.02.2027\nNo seminar\n17.02.2027\nNo seminar\n24.02.2027\nNo seminar\n03.03.2027\n14:00 - 18:00\nPresentations\nPresentation of your seminar paper (20+10mins)\nPrivacy Policy\n|\nLegal Notice\nIf you encounter technical problems, please contact\nthe administrators\n.",
