    "key": "16a90e4ae695981e",
    "title": "Main Page",
    "text": "Main Page\nReproducibility in Fuzzing\nAddison Crump, Moritz Schloegel\nNews\nCurrently, no news are available\nReproducibility in Fuzzing\nFor registration, please apply for this seminar through the\ncentral seminar assignment system.\nBackground:\nScientific papers consist of more than the written document. Their results often depend on research artifacts such as source code, benchmarks, datasets, experimental configurations, documentation, and evaluation scripts. This is particularly important in fuzzing research, where results may be influenced by randomness, benchmark selection, hardware and software environments, execution time, parameter choices, and statistical methodology.\nWhat can you expect?\nIn this seminar, you will study reproducibility and replicability in fuzzing research. You will examine how fuzzing techniques are evaluated, which methodological choices affect their results, and how well published experiments can be reproduced using the accompanying research artifacts.\nThe seminar consists of two parts. In the first, we simulate the work of a Program Committee (PC). Here, you will write four reviews of published fuzzing papers. The goal is to develop your ability to critically read, understand, and evaluate scientific papers. You will also present one of the paper you reviewed, and you are expected to actively participate in discussions of the other papers.\nIn the second part of the seminar, we will model the Artifact Evaluation Committee (AEC), where you will conduct an artifact evaluation of one of the papers you reviewed. This includes installing and examining the provided software, reproducing selected experiments, and assessing the artifact's documentation, functionality, reusability, and support for the claims made in the paper. For this part, you will work in groups of two students. We will provide necessary computation resources. The goal is to reproduce results from recent fuzzing papers while developing a critical understanding of experimental methodology, artifact quality, and reproducibility in computer security research. You will submit a reproduction report documenting your findings and present your findings.\nRequirements:\nThis seminar is intended for advanced bachelor or master students in computer science or related fields. Prior knowledge in the domain of systems security and familiarity with C/C++ is recommended.\nDeliverables:\nThe following deliverables will be the basis of your grade:\nfour reviews of scientific papers\npresentation of one scientific paper and its context\nartifact evaluation report\npresentation of your artifact evaluation report\nactive participation in discussions in the course\nPrivacy Policy\n|\nLegal Notice\nIf you encounter technical problems, please contact\nthe administrators\n.",
