    "text": "Main Page\nTrustworthy Machine Learning\nAdam Dziedzic\nRegistration for this course\nis open until\nMonday, 30.11.2026 23:59\n.\nNews\nPapers for this week: October 24th - October 30th\nWritten on\n24.10.26\nby Adam Dziedzic\nDear Students,\nWe will discuss the following papers on October 30th:\n1. \"Data Provenance Auditing of Fine-Tuned LLMs with a Text-Preserving Technique\"\nICML 2026 –\nhttps://openreview.net/forum?id=3D0x8oxOYH\nThis work introduces invisible Unicode cue–reply marks that let data owners check…\nRead more\nDear Students,\nWe will discuss the following papers on October 30th:\n1. \"Data Provenance Auditing of Fine-Tuned LLMs with a Text-Preserving Technique\"\nICML 2026 –\nhttps://openreview.net/forum?id=3D0x8oxOYH\nThis work introduces invisible Unicode cue–reply marks that let data owners check whether their documents were used to fine-tune an LLM. The method does not change the visible text and has no false positives.\n2.  “Securing Multimodal AI through Internal Information Decomposition” (FlowGuard)\nhttps://openreview.net/forum?id=vTJmIhNvC3\nThe idea of detecting attacks from the inconsistency between unimodal and fused predictions is appealing and the authors leave multi turn and multi image settings explicitly open, which seems like a natural place to build on.\nHave a good weekend,\nAdam Dziedzic\nToday's video\nWritten on\n23.10.26\nby Adam Dziedzic\nDear Students,\nThe video from today was uploaded here: https://youtu.be/-v2qY6XpbHM\nPlease, send me the 4 links to the papers that you would like to present.\nHave a good weekend!\nAdam Dziedzic\nJoin via Zoom\nWritten on\n23.10.26\nby Adam Dziedzic\nDear Students,\nYou can also join via zoom here:\nhttps://cispa-de.zoom-x.de/j/62622206082?pwd=YiGbuJZgPLrzo0Gqcxa93obUJa3XCi.1\nMeeting ID: 626 2220 6082\nPasscode: VW?^R5\nOne tap mobile\n+496938980596,,62622206082# Germany\nDial by your location\n+49 69 389 805 96 Germany\nMeeting ID:…\nRead more\nDear Students,\nYou can also join via zoom here:\nhttps://cispa-de.zoom-x.de/j/62622206082?pwd=YiGbuJZgPLrzo0Gqcxa93obUJa3XCi.1\nMeeting ID: 626 2220 6082\nPasscode: VW?^R5\nOne tap mobile\n+496938980596,,62622206082# Germany\nDial by your location\n+49 69 389 805 96 Germany\nMeeting ID: 626 2220 6082\nFind your local number:\nhttps://cispa-de.zoom-x.de/u/cd6d1pBvMg\nPrivacy Notice:\nPlease refer to\nhttps://cispa.de/en/data-privacy-policy-zoom\nfor our privacy notice regarding the use of Zoom at CISPA\nQuestions for tomorrow's seminar\nWritten on\n22.10.26\nby Adam Dziedzic\nDear Students,\nPlease, send the questions for tomorrow's lecture in the thread on the forum for October 23rd: https://cms.cispa.saarland/tml26/forum/viewtopic.php?t=1\nAs a reminder, we will discuss these 2 papers:\n1. SemBind: https://openreview.net/forum?id=Sva22o2fRs&noteId=QaQG7b76eO\n2.…\nRead more\nDear Students,\nPlease, send the questions for tomorrow's lecture in the thread on the forum for October 23rd: https://cms.cispa.saarland/tml26/forum/viewtopic.php?t=1\nAs a reminder, we will discuss these 2 papers:\n1. SemBind: https://openreview.net/forum?id=Sva22o2fRs&noteId=QaQG7b76eO\n2. Post-hoc Watermarking: https://openreview.net/forum?id=ZR1GOaadgP&noteId=qCnbD0iqOq\nSee you tomorrow,\nAdam Dziedzic\nTimes of the meetings on November 13th and 20th\nWritten on\n20.10.26\nby Adam Dziedzic\nDear All,\nDue to the organization of hackathons, I plan that we will move the meetings on 13th of November to be from 16:00 to 18:00 and on November 20th to be from 12:00 to 14:00.\nWith kind regards,\nAdam Dziedzic\nVideo from Friday's Seminar\nWritten on\n19.10.26\nby Adam Dziedzic\nDear Students,\nThe video for Friday's seminar can be found here: https://youtu.be/zB6lr7xjBmM\nWe will record the videos from the seminars and share publicly on YouTube.\nWith kind regards,\nAdam Dziedzic\nPapers for Next Seminar on October 23rd\nWritten on\n17.10.26\n(last change on\n17.10.26\n)\t\t\t\t\t\tby Adam Dziedzic\nDear Students,\nWe will discuss two paper next week on October 23rd:\n1. SemBind: https://openreview.net/forum?id=Sva22o2fRs&noteId=QaQG7b76eO\n2. Post-hoc Watermarking: https://openreview.net/forum?id=ZR1GOaadgP&noteId=qCnbD0iqOq\nWith kind regards,\nAdam Dziedzic\nShow all\nTrustworthy Machine Learning\nDeploying machine learning in real-world systems necessitates methods to ensure trustworthy AI. This course explores research at the intersection of machine learning, privacy, and security. This course provides a comprehensive overview of techniques to build robust and trustworthy machine learning models, as well as the analysis of data privacy and provenance, focusing on generative models. Throughout the seminar, we will discuss outstanding challenges and future research directions to make machine learning more robust, private, and trustworthy.\nIf you want to register for the seminar, you should do so via the\ncentral seminar assignment system\n.\nTime and place:\n(tentative) The course will take place on Fridays from 2 pm to 4 pm in the CISPA C0 building, room 0.02 (on campus). Some sessions will be via Zoom when the room is not available (part of January) or during main ML conferences (e.g., like NeurIPS in December).\nClass structure:\nIn every class, we will discuss two papers. At the beginning of the semester, students will be assigned roles that will rotate every week. There are three roles:\n1. The Presenters: Two students. Each of them presents a paper and takes the lead in answering the questions posed by the Questioners.\n2. The Questioners: This group is responsible for preparing a list of questions (from each member of the team) about the papers to be discussed in class. For a given week, The Questioners must prepare their questions during the preceding week, and send them to the rest of the class by 5 pm Wednesday. This means that the Questioners must read the papers for their assigned week several days in advance of the actual discussion sessions. We suggest aiming to read the papers by the end of the day on Sunday, to allow at least one day to discuss possible questions.\n3. The Observers: This group will take notes on a shared document during a discussion. These notes are not meant to be a transcription of what is being said in the discussion; they should capture the major take-away points of the discussion, as well as any issues. The Observers should also search for additional resources or answers to unresolved questions.\nThese roles do not preclude anyone in the class from participating in the discussion. A member of The Observers can jump in when a question is posed, and a presenter can pose a new question on the fly.\nProject:\nThe seminars also incorporate project work to foster research skills and creativity. Students begin by selecting papers from the field of trustworthy machine learning that align with their interests. They present these papers and design a project with the potential to evolve into a scientific publication. We encourage you to think critically, identifying weak assumptions in prior work, exploring ways to improve existing methods, combining ideas from multiple sources, or integrating your own experiences and insights from the seminar discussions. Each student writes a comprehensive report detailing their findings and includes code to validate their results. At the end of the semester, you present your projects to the entire class, showcasing the progress and innovations. Use the following\nlatex template\nfor the report.\nRequirements:\nThe course presumes a good understanding of machine learning. The students should have taken and passed a machine learning course and obtained a good grade (<2.0). This seminar is open to senior Bachelor, Master, and Doctoral students. Through seminal and recent papers, students will survey the emerging literature across research communities investigating these issues. The class aims to inspire new research directions and applications. Lectures, slides, and research papers comprise the course materials - no textbook is required. By engaging with the latest work in this rapidly evolving field, students will be prepared to advance trustworthy machine learning. Each student will present one or two papers during the seminar hours in the form of an oral presentation. In addition, each student will read the relevant papers for the other students’ presentations, and hand in a seminar paper summarizing their project at the end of the semester. There will be two sessions when each student presents their project for 10 minutes.\nGrading\nGrading scheme:\n50% as presenters (paper presentation, slide deck, and in-class discussion), 50% for the research project (design a task for the TML course or a hackathon) with a report and a poster. Bonus points: 10% weekly questions and comments as Questioners, 5% as Observers/Scribes, who write a report after each lecture and resolve any unsolved issues.\nClass participation:\nCourse lectures will be driven by the contents of assigned papers. However, students are going to be required to (i) turn in at least 1 question per paper each week as a Questioner and actively participate in the discussion, (ii) present a paper and participate in discussions of the paper content during each class as a Presenter, and (iii) prepare the notes after the class and comment on the slides as the Observer. Hence, the student's ability to exhibit comprehension of papers is essential to a passing grade.\nLateness policy:\nQuestions submissions are assigned each week and presentations will not be accepted late (students will be assigned a 0 for that week). All other assignments (i.e., class or project reports) will be assessed a 10% per day late penalty, up to a maximum of 2 days. Students with legitimate reasons who contact the professor before the deadline may apply for an extension.\nIntegrity:\nStudents have to behave ethically.\nDetailed list of rooms and papers for the seminar will be decided during the first meeting.\nOnline Communication:\nvia Forum (here on CMS).\nEmail:\nadam.dziedzic@cispa.de\nPlan of Seminars\nPlan of Seminars\nDate\nPresentation 1\nPresentation 2\nVideo\nOctober 16th, 2026\nAdam Dziedzic: Introduction and Overview of the Research Topcis\nKaif Shaikh: \"Benchmarking Membership Privacy Risks in Preference-Based LLM Post-Training\" NeurIPS 2026 https://adam-dziedzic.com/static/assets/papers/PrivacyAuditPreferLLMs.pdf\nhttps://youtu.be/zB6lr7xjBmM\nOctober 23rd, 2026\nMahmoud Akrm: SemBind ICML 2026 https://openreview.net/forum?id=Sva22o2fRs&noteId=QaQG7b76eO\nAditya Kasliwal: Post-hoc Watermarking ICML 2026: https://openreview.net/forum?id=ZR1GOaadgP&noteId=qCnbD0iqOq\nhttps://youtu.be/-v2qY6XpbHM\nOctober 30th, 2026\nMuhammad Saad Haroon: \"Data Provenance Auditing of Fine-Tuned LLMs with a Text-Preserving Technique\" ICML 2026\nYogesh Kishor Kulkarni:\n\"Securing Multimodal AI through Internal Information Decomposition” (FlowGuard) ICML 2026\nNovember 6th, 2026\nPresentations of the research projects: each Student presents for 10 minutes\nPresentations of the research projects: each Student presents for 10 minutes\nNovember 13th, 2026\nTBD\nTBD\nNovember 20th, 2026\nTBD\nTBD\nNovember 27th, 2026\nBaozhu Cai:\nGLEAN: Guideline-Grounded Evidence Accumulation for High-Stakes Agent Verification ICML 2026\nhttps://proceedings.mlr.press/v306/zhang26fc.html\nJanuary 8th, 2027\nJanuary 15th, 2027\nJanuary 22nd, 2027\nJanuary 29th, 2027\nFebruary 5th, 2027\nPresentation of the research projects: each Student presents for 10 minutes\nPrivacy Policy\n|\nLegal Notice\nIf you encounter technical problems, please contact\nthe administrators\n.",
