    "text": "Hauptseite\nUsable Security in the Last Decade\nDivyanshu Bhardwaj, Sumair Ijaz Hashmi, Katharina Krombholz, Taha Taha\nRegistration for this course\nis open until\nTuesday, 27.10.2026 23:59\n.\nNews\n27th October Session + Module Preference Form\nWritten on\n21.10.26\nby Taha Taha\nHello everyone,\nNext week, we will go over techniques for reading research papers and discuss the structure of a research paper. Please read the\ntwo compulsory readings\nin the CMS before the session.\nNo\ncritique is due for the next session.\nPlease submit your module preferences using this form:\n…\nRead more\nHello everyone,\nNext week, we will go over techniques for reading research papers and discuss the structure of a research paper. Please read the\ntwo compulsory readings\nin the CMS before the session.\nNo\ncritique is due for the next session.\nPlease submit your module preferences using this form:\nhttps://cispa.qualtrics.com/jfe/form/SV_2nu1YrpeJmPtq62\nDeadline:\nTuesday, 27th October\nBefore filling it out, we recommend skimming the abstracts of the readings in the modules that interest you. Then select your top 3 preferences, in order, in the Qualtrics form. Please note we will do our best to assign you your most preferred module, but we cannot guarantee your first choice. Moreover, preferences cannot be changed once the form is submitted, so please choose carefully. Lastly, please submit the form only once. Duplicate responses will not be accepted.\nIf you have any questions, feel free to reach us at proseminar_usable_security@cispa.de\nSee you next week!\nContent\nThis course explores usable security and privacy, a field that examines how people interact with security & privacy technologies, and how those technologies can be designed to actually work for them. Across the semester we will read and discuss papers from the last decade (2016-2026) covering subfields, such as security advice, authentication, scams & fraud, privacy & consent, at-risk users, and emerging threats to name a few. Students will learn to read papers critically, connect findings across the broader field, and present research effectively to their peers.\nTime & Location\nThe seminar meets weekly on\nTuesdays, 10:00 am – 12:00 pm\nLocation:\nCISPA\nC0 - 0.01 Presentation Room, Stuhlsatzenhaus 5, 66123 Saarbrücken.\nLearning Outcomes\nStudents will learn:\nAbout topics usable security and privacy delves into\nTo read papers effectively\nTo analyze papers\nTo present papers\nGrading\nResearch paper critiques: 40%\nPaper presentation: 40%\nClass participation: 10%\nAttendance: 10%\nClass Structure\nThe course runs as weekly, module-based sessions. Each module corresponds to one subfield within usable security and privacy.\nATTENDANCE IS MANDATORY. If you cannot attend on these dates, please don't register for the seminar.\nCritiques\nEach week's module comes with 3 readings: 2 compulsory and 1 optional. Every week, students will be required to submit a critique on\none\nof the compulsory readings for that week's module. The critique for the week will be due on\nMondays 3 PM\nbefore every session. Since each session happens on a\nTuesday\n, and the critique is due the\nMonday before\nat\n3 PM\n, that means the deadline falls on the day\nbefore\nthe week's session.\nAll students submit a critique on one of the compulsory readings via CMS AND publicly in a discussion forum before the presentation day.\nInstructions on how to write a critique will be provided in the first few sessions and will be made available in the Materials tab.\nPresentations\nEvery module will be a student-led discussion and presentation of that week's\ncompulsory\nreadings. One student presents the\ncompulsory\nreadings for that module. As pointed out in the section above, each week's module comes with 3 readings: 2 compulsory and 1 optional. Rather than presenting each paper separately, the presenter is expected to synthesize\nthe compulsory readings\nof that week's readings into a single, cohesive talk. The presentation should draw connections across the papers, weaving them into one narrative about the module's topic rather than summarizing them one by one.\nThe presenter may incorporate classmates' critiques into their presentation, if they choose to. Students are encouraged, but not required, to conduct additional research on current affairs and additional papers to strengthen their talk as they see fit.\nStudents will be judged on content, clarity, interactiveness, ability to tackle questions, and engagement with external materials.\nMore details on how to present the module, including an example presentation, will be provided in the sessions. A grading rubric will also be made available in the Materials tab.\nSession Breakdown\nDate\nModule\nReadings\nSession Lead\nOctober 20, 2026\nIntroduction Lecture\n—\nCourse Team\n27 Oct 2026\nReading and Analyzing Papers\nCompulsory\nHow to read a paper\nCompulsory\nEnd User and Expert Perceptions of Threats and Potential Countermeasures\n—\nIEEE Euro S&P Workshop 2020\nCourse Team\n3 Nov 2026\nLearning How to Criticize a Paper\nCompulsory\nUse the Force: Evaluating Force-Sensitive Authentication for Mobile Devices\n—\nSOUPS 2016\nCourse Team\nNovember 10, 2026\nHate & Harassment\n(model presentation)\nCompulsory\n“It's common and a part of being a content creator”: Understanding How Creators Experience and Cope with Hate and Harassment Online\n—\nCHI 2022\nCompulsory\n“They Don't Leave Us Alone Anywhere We Go”: Gender and Digital Abuse in South Asia\n—\nCHI 2019\nOptional\n“A Stalker's Paradise”: How Intimate Partner Abusers Exploit Technology\n—\nCHI 2018\nCourse Team\nNovember 17, 2026\nScams & Fraud\nCompulsory\nUsers Really Do Plug in USB Drives They Find\n—\nIEEE S&P 2016\nCompulsory\nUsers Really Do Answer Telephone Scams\n—\nUSENIX Security 2019\nOptional\nExperiences with Digital Scams Post-Incarceration in the US\n—\nSOUPS 2026\n—\nNovember 24, 2026\nSecurity Advice\nCompulsory\nHow I Learned to be Secure: a Census-Representative Survey of Security Advice Sources and Behavior\n—\nCCS 2016\nCompulsory\n“I Wonder if These Warnings Are Accurate”: Security and Privacy Advice in Nine Majority World Countries\n—\nUSENIX Security 2026\nOptional\n“…No one can hack my Mind”: Comparing Expert and Non-Expert Security Practices\n—\nSOUPS 2015\n—\n1 Dec 2026\nAuthentication & Credentials\nCompulsory\nBetter Managed than Memorized? Studying the Impact of Managers on Password Strength and Reuse\n—\nUSENIX Security 2018\nCompulsory\nIs FIDO2 the Kingslayer of User Authentication? A Comparative Usability Study of FIDO2 Passwordless Authentication\n—\nIEEE S&P 2020\nOptional\nA Usability Study of Five Two-Factor Authentication Methods\n—\nSOUPS 2019\n—\nDecember 8, 2026\nResearch Methods\nCompulsory\nReplication: “No One Can Hack My Mind” – 10 Years Later: An Update and Outlook on Experts' and Non-Experts' Security Practices and Advice\n—\nSOUPS 2025\nCompulsory\nHow Well Do My Results Generalize? Comparing Security and Privacy Survey Results from MTurk, Web, and Telephone Samples\n—\nIEEE S&P 2019\nOptional\nDifferent Researchers, Different Results? Analyzing the Influence of Researcher Experience and Data Type During Qualitative Analysis of an Interview and Survey Study on Security Advice\n—\nCHI 2023\n—\nDecember 15, 2026\nPrivacy & Consent\nCompulsory\n(Un)informed Consent: Studying GDPR Consent Notices in the Field\n—\nCCS 2019\nCompulsory\nDark Patterns after the GDPR: Scraping Consent Pop-ups and Demonstrating Their Influence\n—\nCHI 2020\nOptional\nAn Empirical Analysis of Data Deletion and Opt-Out Choices on 150 Websites\n—\nSOUPS 2019\n—\nChristmas Break: 21 December 2026 - 3 January 2027\nJanuary 5, 2027\nAt-Risk Users\nCompulsory\n“\nIn Eighty Percent of the Cases, I Select the Password for Them”: Security and Privacy Challenges, Advice, and Opportunities at Cybercafes in Kenya\n—\nIEEE S&P 2024\nCompulsory\nSoK: A Framework for Unifying At-Risk User Research\n—\nIEEE S&P 2022\nOptional\nHow WEIRD Is Usable Privacy and Security Research?\n—\nUSENIX Security 2024\n—\nJanuary 12, 2027\nEmerging Threats\nCompulsory\n“Did They F***ing Consent to That?”: Safer Digital Intimacy via Proactive Protection Against Image-Based Sexual Abuse\n—\nUSENIX Security 2024\nCompulsory\nMental Models, Expectations and Implications of Client-Side Scanning: An Interview Study with Experts\n—\nCHI 2024\nOptional\nIn Focus, Out of Privacy: The Wearer's Perspective on the Privacy Dilemma of Camera Glasses\n—\nCHI 2024\n—\nJanuary 19, 2027\nDevelopers & Experts\nCompulsory\n“If HTTPS Were Secure, I Wouldn't Need 2FA”: End User and Administrator Mental Models of HTTPS\n—\nIEEE S&P 2020\nCompulsory\nComparing the Usability of Cryptographic APIs\n—\nIEEE S&P 2017\nOptional\nTo Cloud or Not to Cloud: A Qualitative Study on Self-Hosters' Motivation, Operation, and Security Mindset\n—\nUSENIX Security 2023\n—\nJanuary 26, 2027\nFree Lecture\n—\n—\nAcademic Dishonesty\nIn this course, you will be reading a wide range of scholarly sources and will work to integrate these concepts into your own research, critiques and professional practice. It is imperative that any use of others' intellectual work (including, but not limited to, quotations, paraphrasing of ideas, figures, models) be accompanied by an appropriate citation or reference. The course follows Saarland University and DFG's guidelines regarding academic dishonesty: https://www.uni-saarland.de/en/administration/research-integrity.html\nAny form of cheating will result in a penalty up to automatic fail grade for the course. All instances of cheating will be forwarded to the associated university body for appropriate disciplinary action.\nHelping another student to cheat and cheating are considered equal cases of academic dishonesty and will be treated as outlined above.\nGiving another student an electronic copy of your homework, or access to your computer account constitutes cheating on your behalf if that other student copies or uses any files that become implicated in a cheating case.\nAI Policy\nThe use of AI tools (eg, ChatGPT, Claude, or similar) is\nstrictly prohibited\nfor all coursework in this seminar, including critiques and presentations. All critiques and presentation content must be the student's own original work and analysis. Any case of plagiarism will be reported directly to the Examination Office.\nAccessibility\nIf you anticipate or experience physical or academic barriers based on disability, you are welcome to let us know so that we can discuss options (such as live captions during class). You are also encouraged to contact the Equal Opportunities and Diversity Management Unit at: ksb@uni-saarland.de.\nStudent with Disabilities\nIf you have a disability that requires special academic accommodation, please make an appointment to speak with us within the first three (3) weeks of the semester in order to discuss any adjustments. It is important that we talk about this at the beginning of the semester. It is the student's responsibility to notify the Equal Opportunities and Diversity Management Unit (https://www.uni-saarland.de/en/administration/diversity/inclusive-education.html) of an impairment/condition that may require accommodations and/or classroom modifications.\nMental Health\nIf you find yourself beginning to feel some stress, anxiety and/or slightly feeling overwhelmed, please contact the counseling service made available to students through the university: https://www.stw-saarland.de/beratung/\nHarassment Policy\nWe are committed to providing a safe and secure classroom environment for our students. Any kind of harassment or violent behavior is strictly prohibited and will be promptly reported to the Gender Equality Office (https://www.uni-saarland.de/en/administration/gender-equality-office.html).\nQuestions\nWe will make use of a discussion forum to ask questions about the course modules and for students to submit their critiques publicly. We encourage all questions to be asked via this discussion forum so that everyone can learn from each other. For questions that you are not comfortable asking publicly, please reach out at the following email address:\nproseminar_usable_security@cispa.de\nPrivacy Policy\n|\nLegal Notice\nIf you encounter technical problems, please contact\nthe administrators\n.",
