    "title": "Main Page",
    "text": "Main Page\nCausal XAI\nNektarios Kalampalikis, Kunchangtai Liang, María Martínez-García, Isabel Valera\nNews\nPreference Form is now Available\nWritten on\n20.10.26\nby Nektarios Kalampalikis\nDear students,\nthe google form is now open for you to state your preference. You can find the link in the description of the course on CMS. The google form will stop accepting responses on the night of Oct. 27, i.e., after the second background session. If any of you are already sure on which…\nRead more\nDear students,\nthe google form is now open for you to state your preference. You can find the link in the description of the course on CMS. The google form will stop accepting responses on the night of Oct. 27, i.e., after the second background session. If any of you are already sure on which topic you would like to work, you can send your response right now. Otherwise, you can do it after the second session :)\nBest,\nNektarios\nCausal XAI\nThis seminar explores the intersection of causality, explainability, and interpretability in modern machine learning. While traditional methods often rely on statistical associations to understand model behavior, causal approaches aim to uncover the underlying cause-and-effect mechanisms that drive predictions and decision-making. The seminar explores the promises and challenges of causality in Explainable AI (XAI) through the following thematic blocks:\nBlock I: Counterfactual explanations and Causal Interventions\nBlock II: Attribution Methods and Causality\nBlock III: Concept Bottlenecks and Causality\nBlock IV: Representation learning and causality\nStudents will be divided into four teams (one team per seminar block). Each team will work collaboratively throughout the seminar, guided by the instructors, to conduct a literature review on their assigned topic. The final goal of each team is to prepare and deliver a tutorial that introduces the topic, explains its evolution over time, and discusses the current state of the art.\nEvaluation will be based on participation, the quality of the presentations, and the depth of the literature analysis.\nKey sources include:\nInterpretable Machine Learning: A Guide for Making Black Box Models Explainable (3rd ed.)\nLimitations of Interpretable Machine Learning Methods\nTime and place\nTuesdays\n, 12:15 to 13:45\nRoom\n: 2.06 at E1.1\nBoth Private Meetings and Public presentations will be happening at 2.06 at E1.1.\nAttendance\nThe seminar will be held in person, and\nattendance is mandatory\n.\nGeneral structure\nThe students will be divided into four teams (one team per seminar block), with three students per team. Each team will work collaboratively throughout the seminar, guided by the instructors, to conduct a literature review on their assigned topic. The final goal is for each group to prepare and deliver a tutorial that introduces the topic, explains how it has evolved over time, and discusses the current state of the art.\nThe seminar will include three types of sessions:\nBackground sessions:\nSessions led by the instructors to provide the necessary foundations and guidance on how to approach and understand research papers.\nPrivate group meetings:\nMeetings with each team to assess their understanding, discuss their planned presentation, identify missing elements or unclear aspects, and provide feedback on content, structure, and their understanding of the research landscape.\nThese meetings are not rehearsals\n: students are expected to understand and explain their material and answer questions.\nPublic presentations:\nSessions in which each team delivers its final tutorial to the rest of the class, followed by questions and discussion.\nCalendar\nDate\nSession Type\nContent\nOctober 20\nBackground\nIntroduction to Causality\nOctober 27\nBackground\nIntroduction to Explainability\nOctober 28\nDeadline\nStudents assigned to blocks\nOctober 29\nOptional - Background\nHow to read a paper?\nin Room 0.01, Building E1.7\nfrom 12:15 to 13:45\n2 weeks without seminar sessions\nNovember 10\nDeadline\nLSF Registration Deadline\nNovember 17\nPrivate meetings (1, 2)\nBuilding a tutorial I\nNovember 24\nPrivate meetings (3, 4)\nBuilding a tutorial I\n1 week without seminar sessions\nDecember 8\nPublic presentations (1, 2)\nBuilding a tutorial I\nDecember 15\nPublic presentations (3, 4)\nBuilding a tutorial I\n3 weeks without seminar sessions\nJanuary 12\nPrivate meetings (1, 2)\nBuilding a tutorial II\nJanuary 19\nPrivate meetings (3, 4)\nBuilding a tutorial II\n1 week without seminar sessions\nFebruary 2\nPublic presentations (1, 2)\nBuilding a tutorial II\nFebruary 9\nPublic presentations (3, 4)\nBuilding a tutorial II\n* Each team has 2 weeks between the private group meeting and the presentation, and 4 weeks between their first presentation and the next private meeting.\nSelecting topic\nWe will share a form with the students to indicate their preferences.\nGoogle Form Link\nRelease form to indicate preferences:\nOctober 20 (first background session) - until October 27\nRelease team assignment:\nOctober 28 (second background session, 3 weeks before the first private group meeting)\nDeliverables and grading scheme\nEach team is expected to prepare presentation slides for the private meetings and public presentations. The private meetings provide an opportunity to assess students' understanding and give feedback to improve the final public presentations. Students should arrive prepared to explain and discuss their material; the meetings are not rehearsals.\nIndividual responsibility.\nAlthough the presentations are prepared in groups, each member is expected to understand the material they are responsible for and contribute to the discussion. Each group must also provide a brief\ncontribution statement\nspecifying the work carried out by each member, including their contributions to the literature review and presentation preparation.\nGrading:\nEach of the four meetings will be assessed according to its corresponding grading scheme, covering content quality, understanding, critical analysis, presentation quality, and participation as applicable.\nSession Type\nDeliverables\nPoints\nPrivate meeting – Tutorial I\nSlides + list of limitations\n15\nPublic presentations – Tutorial I\nSlides + contribution statement\n30\nPrivate meeting – Tutorial II\nSlides + list of papers (connected to the gaps)\n15\nPublic presentations – Tutorial II\nSlides + contribution statement\n40\nTotal\n–\n100\nTo get an excellent grade you need to:\nExplain, not just describe:\nShow that you understand the intuition behind concepts and methods.\nBe critical:\nDiscuss assumptions, limitations, and unresolved problems rather than simply summarizing papers.\nConnect ideas:\nBuild a clear narrative from the initial approaches to later developments and remaining challenges.\nSupport your arguments:\nUse concrete examples and evidence from the literature.\nDemonstrate ownership\n: Be prepared to defend your choices, explain your assigned papers, and answer questions.\nDetailed structure\nBackground sessions (2+1 sessions)\nThe seminar will start with two background sessions designed to give students the necessary tools to begin their literature review. An additional,\noptional session\non how to read academic papers will be offered by our chair on\nOctober 29, from 12:15 to 13:45, in Room 0.01, Building E1.7\n. The paper\n[A Simple Framework for Contrastive Learning of Visual Representations]\nwill be used as an example. Please note that this room is shared with another seminar, which takes priority. As a result, seating is limited, and availability cannot be guaranteed.\nBuilding a tutorial I: Foundations, Definitions, and Research Questions (4 sessions)\nThe goal of this stage is to establish a strong understanding of the foundations of the assigned topic. Students should introduce the key concepts, explain the foundational approaches in the literature, and identify the limitations and open questions that motivated subsequent research.\nPrivate group meetings (2 sessions)\nOne private meeting per team (45 min).\nTwo teams per session.\nDeliverables and expectations:\nBriefly walk the instructors through the slides planned for the first public presentation, covering the motivation, key concepts, foundational approaches, and research gaps.\nThis is not a rehearsal!\nThe goal is to review the slides and explain what the team plans to present and why.\nExplain the material clearly and demonstrate an understanding of the underlying concepts and methods.\nBe prepared to answer questions and discuss the limitations and assumptions of the approaches presented.\nUse the feedback received to improve the content and structure of the public presentation.\nPublic presentation (2 sessions)\nOne presentation per team (30 min for presentation + 15 min for Q&A).\nTwo presentations per session.\nThe presentation should cover\n:\nMotivation and problem framing\n:\nWhy the topic matters, its applications, and the problem it addresses.\nKey definitions and concepts\n:\nCorrect explanations, supported by intuition and examples.\nFoundational approaches\n:\nThe main initial methods, their core ideas, and how they differ.\nLimitations and research gaps\n:\nThe assumptions and limitations of the foundational approaches, and the open questions they leave unresolved.\nBuilding a Tutorial II: Evolution of the Topic and Current State of the Art (4 sessions)\nBuilding on Tutorial I, students should expand their presentation to explain how the field has evolved, how subsequent research has addressed the initial gaps, and what remains unresolved. The presentation should cover major developments, influential works, recent advances, and current research challenges. Teams should also develop their own perspective and draw reasoned conclusions.\nTutorial II should not feel like a second, longer version of Tutorial I. While Tutorial I establishes the foundations and identifies research gaps, Tutorial II explains how the field has responded to those gaps and critically assesses its current state.\nEach team is expected to discuss\nat least 9 papers (a minimum of 3 papers per team member)\n, with possible exceptions for specific seminar blocks.\nPrivate group meetings (2 sessions)\nOne private meeting per team (45 minutes).\nDeliverables and expectations:\nPresent the planned slides for Tutorial II, including a revised introduction incorporating feedback from Tutorial I.\nDiscuss the selected papers and explain how they relate to the research gaps identified in Tutorial I.\nExplain the contributions, methods, assumptions, results, and limitations of the selected papers.\nIdentify remaining open questions and develop a critical perspective on the literature.\nBe prepared to discuss the material in depth and answer questions.\nPublic presentation (2 sessions)\nOne presentation per team (35 min for presentation + 10 min for Q&A)\nThe presentation should cover\nRecap\n:\nA concise review of the essential foundations and research gaps from Tutorial I.\nEvolution of the field\n:\nA coherent storyline showing how the literature developed and how papers build on, differ from, or improve upon earlier work.\nAnalysis of individual papers\n:\nThe problem addressed, core method, intuition, assumptions, key results, and limitations of each paper.\nRemaining challenges\n:\nSpecific open questions that follow from the literature.\nOwn perspective and conclusions\n:\nA reasoned assessment of what has been achieved, what remains unresolved, and which future directions are promising.\nTopics\nBlock 1 – Counterfactuals and Interventions\nStudents will explore techniques for counterfactual reasoning and causal interventions, including counterfactual explanations, causal recourse, and feature-effect visualization methods. They will learn why ignoring causality can be misleading and how causal models enable intervention-consistent and actionable recommendations.\nMain instructor:\nRickey\nKeywords\n: counterfactual explanations, causal recourse, causal interventions, Partial Dependence Plots (PDPs), Ceteris Paribus Plots.\nSources - Seminal work:\nInterpretable Machine Learning: A Guide for Making Black Box Models Explainable (3rd ed.)\nLimitations of Interpretable Machine Learning Methods\nCounterfactual explanations without opening the black box: Automated decisions and the GDPR\n.\nOn the relationship between explanation and prediction: A causal view\n.\nAlgorithmic recourse: from counterfactual explanations to interventions\n.\nBlock 2 – Attribution methods and Causality\nThis block introduces fundamental post-hoc explainability methods, discusses their limitations, and interprets these limitations in causal terms. It further explores how causal reasoning can improve attribution methods.\nMain instructor:\nNektarios\nKeywords\n: feature importance, variable importance under hidden confounding, SHAP values, causality\nSupplementary topics\n: Machine Learning - Additive Models (GAMs), Influential instances, influence functions, prototypes and criticisms, local surrogate models (LIME)\nSources - Seminal work:\nInterpretable Machine Learning: A Guide for Making Black Box Models Explainable (3rd ed.)\nLimitations of Interpretable Machine Learning Methods\nA unified approach to interpreting model predictions.\nhttps://shap.readthedocs.io/en/latest/\n, or any other of the\nplethora\nof sources for SHAP.\nhttps://scikit-learn.org/stable/modules/permutation_importance.html\nCausal shapley values: Exploiting causal knowledge to explain individual predictions of complex models\n.\nCausal SHAP: Feature Attribution with Dependency Awareness through Causal Discovery\n.\nTHE FAILURES OF MARGINAL INFLUENCE-BASED ATTRIBUTION METHODS FOR GLOBAL TIME SERIES EXPLANATIONS\nDoctor Rashomon and the UNIVERSE of Madness: Variable Importance with Unobserved Confounding and the Rashomon Effect.\nBlock 3 – Concept Bottlenecks and Causality\nIntroduce concept-based neural networks, such as concept bottleneck models, present their limitations and see how to introduce causality into them to alleviate their problems.\nMain instructor:\nNektarios\nKeywords\n: concept bottleneck models, concept-based explanations, causal CBMs\nSources - Seminal work:\nConcept bottleneck models\nCausally reliable concept bottleneck models\nTowards reasonable concept bottleneck models.\nCounterfactual concept bottleneck models.\nBlock 4 – Representation learning and Causality\nHow to move beyond statistical correlations toward representations that capture the underlying (interpretable) causal structure of the data.\nMain instructor:\nRickey\nKeywords\n: causal representation learning, spurious correlations, interventions, causal concept-Based Representation Learning\nSources - Seminal work:\nRepresentation learning: A review and new perspectives.\nToward causal representation learning\n.\nBeta-VAE: Learning basic visual concepts with a constrained variational framework\nFrom causal to concept-based representation learning\n.\nRequirements\nThis seminar is primarily aimed at master's students in Computer Science, Data Science and Artificial Intelligence (DSAI), or related fields, who have prior knowledge of machine learning. Participants are expected to have completed one or more courses related to machine learning, such as Machine Learning, Elements of Machine Learning, Linear Algebra, Neural Networks, or equivalent courses.\nPrior exposure to topics in explainability, causality, or trustworthy AI is beneficial but not strictly required. In particular, the seminars Explainable Machine Learning, Advanced Topics in Causality and Causal Machine Learning Research, Causethical ML and Trustworthy Machine Learning provide valuable background and will help students engage more deeply with the seminar material.\nPrivacy Policy\n|\nLegal Notice\nIf you encounter technical problems, please contact\nthe administrators\n.",
