    "text": "Mathematical Image Analysis Group, Saarland University\nProseminar\nNature-Inspired Optimisation\nWinter Term 2026 / 2027\nHome\nAbout Us\nPeople\nTeaching\nResearch\nPublications\nAwards\nLinks\nContact\nInternal\nInformation\nProeminar: Nature-Inspired Optimisation\nProf. Joachim Weickert\nWinter Term 2026 / 2027\nProseminar (2 h)\nImportant Dates\n–\nDescription\n–\nRegistration\n–\nRequirements\n–\nIntroductory Meeting\n–\nOverview of Topics\nImportant Dates\nIntroductory meeting (mandatory):\nThe introductory meeting has been postponed to Wednesday, October 28, \n2026, 16:15 via Zoom. The link will be sent to all participants who \nhave registered.\nIn this meeting, we will assign the topics to the participants.\nAttendance is\nmandatory\nfor all participants. \nDo not forget to register first (see below).\nRegular meetings during the winter term 2026 / 2027:\nWednesday 16:15-18:00, online via our Zoom link.\nDate of first regular meeting:\nTo be announced.\nLast meeting:\nTo be announced.\nWrite-up submission deadline:\nFebruary 28, 2027, 23:59. Please e-mail your write-up to\nJoachim Weickert.\nDescription\nContents:\nIn millions of years, nature has developed a wide variety of survival\nstrategies. Many of them are simple, but still allow to tackle complex\nproblems and tasks. In this proseminar, we will explore algorithms\ninspired by nature that can be used to solve optimisation problems.\nThis includes e.g. genetic algorithms and methods inspired by swarm\nbehavior. The seminar is based on the book \"Nature-Inspired\nMetaheuristic Algorithms\" by Xin-She Yang.\nPrerequisites:\nThe proseminar is designed for bachelor students of a mathematics\nor computer science study program. They are supposed to have some basic\nmathematical knowledge (e.g. Mathematics for Computer Scientists I-III)\nLanguage:\nAll papers are written in English, and English is the language of presentation.\nRegistration\nPlease register via the\nSIC Registration\n  Website\nby October 14, 2026, 20:00 CET.\nRequirements for successful participation («Schein«)\nRegular attendance:\nYou must attend all seminar meetings, except for provable important reasons \nsuch as illness (medical certificate required).\nTalk:\nTalk duration is 30 min, plus 15 min for discussion. Please do not\ndeviate from this time schedule.\nYour presentation must be given in English. Your slides and your \nwrite-up, too, should be in English.\nWrite-up:\nThe write-up has to be submitted until Sunday, February 28, 2027, 23:59 \nvia e-mail to Joachim Weickert.\nIt should summarise your talk on 5 pages.\nThe file format for electronic submissions is PDF – text \nprocessor files (like .doc) are not acceptable.\nParticipants who do not submit a writeup cannot obtain the certificate \nfor the seminar.\nPlagiarism:\nPlease adhere to the standards of scientific referencing and avoid \nplagiarism: Quotations and copied material (such as images) must be \nclearly marked as such, and a bibliography is required.\nOtherwise the seminar counts as failed.\nMandatory consultation:\nTalk preparation has to be presented to your seminar supervisor no later \nthan one week\nbefore\nthe talk is given. \nIt is your responsibility to approach us timely and make your appointment.\nNo-shows:\nNo-shows are unfair to your fellow students: Some talks are based on\nprevious talks, and your seminar place might have prevented the\nparticipation of another student. Thus, in case you do not appear to \nyour scheduled talk (except for reasons beyond your control), we \nreserve the right to exclude you from future seminars of our group.\nParticipation in discussions:\nThe discussions after the presentations are a vital part of this seminar. This \nmeans that the audience (i.e. all paricipants) poses questions and tries to \nfind positive and negative aspects of the proposed concept and provides\nfeedback and constructive criticism regarding the presentation. This \nactive participation is part of your final grade.\nBeing in time:\nYou are expected to participate from the beginning to the end.\nIntroductory Meeting\nHere you will find the\nslides from the introductory meeting\n.\nThey contain important information for preparing a good talk.\nTopics\nWe will discuss the chapters below. If your registration was successful, \nthe password will be sent to you before the introductory meeting. This \nwill enable you to inspect the topics and identify your favourites.\nNo.\nDate\nSpeaker\nPaper\n1\nTBD\nN.N.\nSlides\nChapter 2: Random Walks and LÃ©vy Flights\n2\nTBD\nN.N.\nSlides\nChapter 3: Simulated Annealing\n3\nTBD\nN.N.\nSlides\nChapter 4: How to Deal with Constraints\n4\nTBD\nN.N.\nSlides\nChapter 5: Genetic Algorithms\n5\nTBD\nN.N.\nSlides\nChapter 6: Differential Evolution\n6\nTBD\nN.N.\nSlides\nChapter 7: Ant and Bee Algorithms\n7\nTBD\nN.N.\nSlides\nChapter 8: Swarm Optimisation\n8\nTBD\nN.N.\nSlides\nChapter 9: Harmony Search\n9\nTBD\nN.N.\nSlides\nChapter 10: Firefly Algorithm\n10\nTBD\nN.N.\nSlides\nChapter 11: Bat Algorithm\n11\nTBD\nN.N.\nSlides\nChapter 12: Cuckoo Search\n12\nTBD\nN.N.\nSlides\nChapter 13: ANNs and Support Vector Machines\nMIA Group\n©2001-2023\nThe author is not\nresponsible for\nthe content of\nexternal pages.\nImprint\n-\nData protection",
