    "text": "Theoretical Abilities and Limitations of Language Models | LaCoCo Lab\nLaCoCo Lab\nTheoretical Abilities and Limitations of Language Models\nCourse Description:\nLLM have amazing capabilities, but also hallucinate and make reasoning mistakes. Can we understand these abilities and limitations theoretically, as way to figure out ways of overcoming them?\nThe incredible scale of current LLMs makes this a daunting prospect. However, recent research has developed mathematical understanding sheeding light on questions such as:\nWhy do LLMs struggle with tasks (e.g., multiplying 6-digit numbers) that a simple calculator can perform easily?\nWhich kinds of problems is a transformer likely to generalize on well?\nWhat are differences between transformers and other architectures (e.g. state-space models)?\nWhat possible future architectures – if any – could replace transformers as the backbone of LLMs?\nResearch on these questions draws on fields such as computational complexity, formal language theory, and statistical learning theory.\nPrerequisites:\nThis seminar will not presuppose knowledge of any of these fields. However, a willingness to engage with technical content is\nimportant\n.\nWe will discuss recent research papers, both from our own group and from other groups. A lot of this content is highly technical, and starting early is highly recommended. It is okay to not understand everything in the paper assigned to you, but you are encouraged to ask questions about things that are confusing. We will try to help each other in getting familiar with techniques that are useful establishing such results, and also at the same time make sure everybody is on-board with the high level idea of the papers at the very least.\nCourse Management System:\nCMS\nInstructors:\nYash Sarrof\nTime:\n12:15 - 13:45 (every Friday)\nRoom:\nBuilding C7 3 - Seminarraum 1.14\nRegistration:\nIf you are an\nLST / CoLi\nstudent, and want to take this class, you should directly register in the\nCourse Management System (CMS)\n. Admissions decision will be made by the 20th October.\nIf you are a\nComputer Science\nstudent, you should initially register via the Computer Science department seminar registration system. Only register in\nCourse Management System (CMS)\nonce you were selected by the assignment system or otherwise admitted by us.\nSyllabus\nDate\nSession / core reading\nOptional material\nPresenters\n16 Oct 2026\nCourse introduction\nLogistics, course structure, and expectations.\n—\n23 Oct 2026\nNo class\n—\n30 Oct 2026\nTutorial\nFor presenters of papers on expressivity and length generalization.\n—\nExpressivity\nIn principle, if we could magically hardcode the weights of a transformer, what would they be capable of?\n6 Nov 2026\nTheoretical Limitations of Self-Attention in Neural Sequence Models\nHahn · TACL 2020\nFormal Language Recognition by Hard Attention Transformers: Perspectives from Circuit Complexity\nHao, Angluin & Frank · TACL 2022\nSamuel BK\n13 Nov 2026\nThe Expressive Power of Transformers with Chain of Thought\nMerrill & Sabharwal · ICLR 2024\nAttention is Turing-Complete\nPérez, Barceló & Marinkovic · JMLR 2021\nAftab Khan\nLength Generalization\nWhen do Transformers learn to do a task, at lengths bigger than the maximum training length?\n20 Nov 2026\nRASP:\nThinking Like Transformers\nWeiss, Goldberg & Yahav · ICML 2021\nRASP-L:\nWhat Algorithms can Transformers Learn? A Study in Length Generalization\nZhou et al. · ICLR 2024\nD-RASP:\nDiscovering Interpretable Algorithms by Decompiling Transformers to RASP\nHuang et al. · ICML 2026\nDeng Zhepei\n27 Nov 2026\nA Formal Framework for Understanding Length Generalization in Transformers\nHuang et al. · ICLR 2025\nC-RASP:\nCounting Like Transformers: Compiling Temporal Counting Logic Into Softmax Transformers\nYang & Chiang · COLM 2024\nNazanin Zarei Rad\n4 Dec 2026\nTutorial\nFor rest of the readings\n—\nFine-Grained Theoretical Insights\nCan we get deeper/ more predictive insights into architecture performance?\n11 Dec 2026\nCharacterizing the Expressivity of Fixed-Precision Transformer Language Models\nLi & Cotterell · NeurIPS 2025\nA Logic for Expressing Log-Precision Transformers\nMerrill & Sabharwal · NeurIPS 2023\nMeropi Maria Lampropoulou\n18 Dec 2026\nKnee-Deep in C-RASP: A Transformer Depth Hierarchy\nYang, Cadilhac & Chiang · NeurIPS 2025\nDisentangling the Expressivity of RoPE\nJerad et al. · COLM 2026\nZhanyu Chen\n8 Jan 2027\nAlgebraic Decomposition Theory for Transformer Length Generalization\nYang et al. · COLM 2026\nTransformers Learn Shortcuts to Automata\nLiu et al. · ICLR 2023\nGaspard Tomas\nTowards Better Architectures?\nHow can this kind of theory be useful? Can we make better architectures based on theoretical insights?\n15 Jan 2027\nUnlocking State-Tracking in Linear RNNs Through Negative Eigenvalues\nGrazzi et al. · ICLR 2025\nThe Expressive Capacity of State Space Models: A\nFormal Language Perspective\nSarrof, Veitsman & Hahn · NeurIPS 2024\nPhineas Degener\n22 Jan 2027\nOlmo Hybrid: From Theory to Practice and Back\nMerrill et al. · COLM 2026\nThe Illusion of State in State-Space Models\nMerrill, Petty & Sabharwal · ICML 2024\nAli Alfwari\nWhy Are Transformers Still So Good?\nAn alternate and very new way to think about architectures that very clearly establishes why Transformers are so much better than any alternate architecture that has been used in language modeling, thus far.\n29 Jan 2027\nTransformers are Inherently Succinct\nBergsträßer, Cotterell & Lin · ICLR 2026\n—\nMaike Kalms\nAttendance\nActive participation is expected in every seminar session. You may miss up to two sessions without providing justification. For each additional session missed beyond that, you must submit a short (~500 words) writeup summarizing your thoughts on the paper(s) discussed in the session you missed.\nEvaluation\nImportant: Study programs may differ in which versions of the class you can take. Please check with your study program coordinator if in doubt.\nFor students taking the seminar for 4 credits:\nPresentation: 40%\nQuestions about readings: 30%\nParticipation in class: 30%\nFor students taking the seminar for 7 credits:\nPresentation: 20%\nQuestions about readings: 15%\nParticipation in class: 15%\nFinal paper: 50%\nImportant: You need to be registered on LSF for the correct version of the class in order to receive credit.\nQuestions\nPlease register on the forum on CMS (in CMS, you will see “forum” on the top toolbar, and reply in the forum to post questions).\nWhen the presentations starts, every student submits one question about the readings by Wednesday noon.\nQuestions are graded on a 3-point scale (0: no question submitted, 1: superficial question, 2: insightful question). Students can also submit a few more questions, the grade will be calculated as the highest score among questions.（So you can also ask some basic questions that you want clarification). Try to be direct with your questions, and ask the same question in class, if it hasn’t been answered by the presenter.\nPresentations\nDepending on the difficulty of the reading, we might take 2 weeks to discuss a single paper, or at times do 2 papers in a single week. Feel free to present in any format you like, including a white board presentation if you want. If you want a suggestion, here’s a recommended (albeit new) format.\nShort overview\n: Every presentation should start with, a\n15 minute presentation\ngiving high level details, and the overall idea of the paper. You should also then leave about\n15 minutes for Q&A\n. You are encouraged to combine the Q&A with your presentation, but are not required to do so. In this version of the presentation, you should focus on\nbig picture findings\nand conclusions, and you are not expected to include every finding from the paper.\nFor instance, instead of a table of numbers, highlight key results.\nWhen there are multiple similar results in the paper, synthesize them.\nIf the papers have many studies, you might select a presentative subset to explain the paper’s conclusions.\nOn the other hand, if the assigned papers primarily discuss/review other work, draw on material from the work cited to provide richer content and even details where useful.\nDiscussing a specific claim in detail\n: We won’t have time to discuss all the claims of the paper in detail, and thus try to identify the central claim of the paper, and design and then aim for about\n30 minutes\n, to go into the details of that claim, including explaining the underlying proof, if necessary.\nGive background on key concepts, if necessary to explain whats going on here.\nYour own opinion\n: Finally end the paper, by critically engaging with the reading: contribute your own opinion on the key findings, and on the paper’s motivation and arguments. In what ways do or don’t you agree with arguments made by the authors? Aim for around\n10\nminutes here, leaving the rest of the time for Q&A.\nYou will have sufficient time, so avoid speaking too fast for others to keep up, make sure your audience is following. You can do so by giving more examples, or simply repeating again. Making presentation is to convey information, if audience cannot follow, it’s waste of time for both sides.\nGenerating and moderating in-class discussion is a key component of your presentation – thinking about what will be interesting to your audience will thus be important.\nTry to engage the audience and\ncreate ample opportunity for discussion during your presentation\n.\nBefore the presentation, take a look at the questions that have been posted in the forum and refer to these as needed. These may be useful for getting discussion started.\nConversely, when attending other students’ talks, reciprocate by participating actively in the discussion.\nFinal Papers (for the 7CP version)\nTerm papers will be about a small independent project.\nYou will investigate some question about the abilities of language models. It can be a theoretical work, or an empirical project verifying insights from theoretical work or providing inputs to theoretical work. For example, one may do experiments to show the expressivity or generalization performance of some novel architectures (\n1\n,\n2\n,\n3\n,\n4\n,\n5\n, do not repeat previous work, try to experiment with new architectures or new tasks).\nThe report is expected to contain a brief literature review, motivation of your study, a description of what you did, and you found. It’s recommended to include quantitative validation of what you found, so that you show it’s not an illusion.\nThe report should have 8 pages of main report, plus unlimited appendix, in the NeurIPS style format. The main report should be self-contained, but you can use the appendix to report prompts, further analyses, or other material.\nThe report should be uploaded via CMS. The due date is 23:59 on March 15, 2027.\nContact\nPlease contact Yash (ysarrof@lst.uni-saarland.de) or Michael (mhahn@lst.uni-saarland.de) for any questions.\nAccommodations\nIf you need any accommodations due to a disability or chronic illness, please either contact Michael at mhahn@lst.uni-saarland.de or the\nEqual Opportunities and Diversity Management Unit\nof the university.",
