    "text": "Machine Learning for Molecular and Materials Simulations · WS 2026/27\nSkip to course content\nSaarland University · WS26/27 · Research seminar · Computer Science\nMachine Learning for Molecular and Materials Simulations\nInstructor:\nViktor Zaverkin\nKickoff\nMonday, 19 October 2026\n2:00–3:30 pm\nLecture Hall A\nINM, Campus D2 2\nWe will agree on the regular meeting time after the kickoff, based on participants’ availability.\nRegister here\nFormat\n· Weekly, 90 minutes\nLanguage\n· English\nPlaces\n· 12\nAudience\n· Advanced BSc / MSc\nOn this page\nOverview\nRequirements\nTopics\nFormat\nSchedule\nLinks & contact\nOverview\nMolecular and materials simulations are central tools for understanding and predicting the behavior of complex atomistic systems. However, they are often limited by three key bottlenecks: accurate and efficient force evaluations, sampling of rare configurations, and reaching experimentally relevant time and length scales.\nThis seminar studies how modern machine learning approaches address these bottlenecks through machine-learned interatomic potentials, generative sampling methods, and accelerated molecular simulations.\nTopics include descriptor-based and equivariant potentials, transformer-based and foundation models for atomistic systems, classical and generative approaches to free-energy sampling, and methods for accelerating molecular dynamics. Students will read and discuss recent research papers, present selected methods, and critically assess their strengths, limitations, and physical reliability.\nThe example below shows replica-exchange molecular dynamics (REMD) of multielement clusters in\nLAMMPS\n, with forces predicted by\nFlashCart\n.\nYour browser does not support embedded video.\nDownload the simulation video\n.\nMendeleev clusters.\nPanel borders indicate target temperatures (300–3000 K). Simulation adapted from the\nAtomistic Cookbook example\n.\nRequirements\nA basic background in machine learning and scientific computing, including familiarity with linear algebra and statistics, is expected. Prior knowledge of molecular dynamics, statistical mechanics, quantum mechanics, or free-energy methods is helpful but not required.\nTopics\n(tentative)\nEach participant presents a selected research topic. Topics will be assigned after the kickoff.\nExpand all topics\nInteratomic potentials\n01\nLocal representations\nLocal representations encode an atom’s neighborhood while preserving invariance to translations, rotations, and permutations of atoms of the same species. We will compare atom-centered symmetry functions (ACSFs), the smooth overlap of atomic positions (SOAP), and the atomic cluster expansion (ACE), discussing their differences, advantages, and limitations.\nJ. Behler.\nAtom-centered symmetry functions for constructing high-dimensional neural network potentials\n.\nJournal of Chemical Physics\n134\n, 074106 (2011).\nA. P. Bartók, R. Kondor & G. Csányi.\nOn representing chemical environments\n.\nPhysical Review B\n87\n, 184115 (2013).\nR. Drautz.\nAtomic cluster expansion for accurate and transferable interatomic potentials\n.\nPhysical Review B\n99\n, 014104 (2019).\n02\nInvariant message passing\nInvariant message-passing models learn atomic representations by exchanging information between neighboring atoms while keeping their features unchanged under rotations. We will compare SchNet, which uses interatomic distances, with DimeNet, which also includes angles, discussing their differences, advantages, and limitations.\nK. T. Schütt, H. E. Sauceda, P.-J. Kindermans, A. Tkatchenko & K.-R. Müller.\nSchNet – A deep learning architecture for molecules and materials\n.\nThe Journal of Chemical Physics\n148\n, 241722 (2018).\nJ. Gasteiger, J. Groß & S. Günnemann.\nDirectional Message Passing for Molecular Graphs\n.\nInternational Conference on Learning Representations\n(2020).\n03\nEquivariant message passing\nEquivariant message passing uses features that transform with rotations of the atomic structure. We will compare NequIP and MACE, focusing on their message construction and the advantages and limitations of each approach.\nS. Batzner, A. Musaelian, L. Sun, M. Geiger, J. P. Mailoa, M. Kornbluth, N. Molinari, T. E. Smidt & B. Kozinsky.\nE(3)-equivariant graph neural networks for data-efficient and accurate interatomic potentials\n.\nNature Communications\n13\n, 2453 (2022).\nI. Batatia, D. P. Kovács, G. N. C. Simm, C. Ortner & G. Csányi.\nMACE: Higher Order Equivariant Message Passing Neural Networks for Fast and Accurate Force Fields\n.\nAdvances in Neural Information Processing Systems\n35\n, 11423–11436 (2022).\n04\nUnconstrained architectures\nThe Point Edge Transformer (PET) learns atomic interactions without imposing rotational equivariance in its architecture. Direct force models predict forces without computing them as derivatives of a potential energy. We will discuss the advantages and limitations of both approaches for molecular dynamics.\nS. N. Pozdnyakov & M. Ceriotti.\nSmooth, exact rotational symmetrization for deep learning on point clouds\n.\nAdvances in Neural Information Processing Systems\n36\n, 79469–79501 (2023).\nF. Bigi, M. F. Langer & M. Ceriotti.\nThe dark side of the forces: assessing non-conservative force models for atomistic machine learning\n.\nProceedings of Machine Learning Research\n267\n, 4384–4414 (2025).\n05\nFoundation interatomic potentials\nFoundation interatomic potentials are trained on diverse datasets and can be applied or fine-tuned to new systems. We will discuss MACE-MP-0, focusing on its transferability, advantages, and limitations.\nI. Batatia et al.\nA foundation model for atomistic materials chemistry\n.\nThe Journal of Chemical Physics\n163\n, 184110 (2025).\n06\nLong-range interactions\nElectrostatic interactions can couple atoms beyond the range of local models. We will examine how interatomic potentials describe long-range interactions, charge transfer, and polarization, discussing the advantages and limitations of different approaches.\nW. J. Baldwin, I. Batatia, M. Vondrák, J. T. Margraf & G. Csányi.\nDesign Space of Self-Consistent Electrostatic Machine Learning Interatomic Potentials\n.\narXiv preprint\narXiv:2603.14700 (2026).\nEnhanced and generative sampling\n07\nRare events and enhanced sampling\nFree-energy barriers can prevent molecular dynamics simulations from exploring relevant configurations within accessible timescales. Metadynamics adds a history-dependent bias along selected collective variables to promote transitions between metastable states. We will compare the original and well-tempered formulations, discussing the choice of collective variables and the recovery of free-energy differences.\nA. Laio & M. Parrinello.\nEscaping free-energy minima\n.\nProceedings of the National Academy of Sciences\n99\n, 12562–12566 (2002).\nA. Barducci, G. Bussi & M. Parrinello.\nWell-Tempered Metadynamics: A Smoothly Converging and Tunable Free-Energy Method\n.\nPhysical Review Letters\n100\n, 020603 (2008).\n08\nLearning collective variables\nCollective variables provide a low-dimensional description of molecular configurations. We will compare Deep-LDA, which learns to separate predefined metastable states, with Deep-TICA, which uses time-lagged correlations to learn slow collective modes. The discussion will address their training objectives, data requirements, and use in enhanced sampling.\nL. Bonati, V. Rizzi & M. Parrinello.\nData-Driven Collective Variables for Enhanced Sampling\n.\nThe Journal of Physical Chemistry Letters\n11\n, 2998–3004 (2020).\nL. Bonati, G. Piccini & M. Parrinello.\nDeep learning the slow modes for rare events sampling\n.\nProceedings of the National Academy of Sciences\n118\n, e2113533118 (2021).\n09\nBoltzmann generators\nBoltzmann generators use invertible models to transform samples from a simple distribution into molecular configurations. We will examine energy-based and data-based training, and how reweighting is used to estimate equilibrium properties. The discussion will address sampling efficiency and the challenge of sampling multiple metastable states.\nF. Noé, S. Olsson, J. Köhler & H. Wu.\nBoltzmann generators: Sampling equilibrium states of many-body systems with deep learning\n.\nScience\n365\n, eaaw1147 (2019).\n10\nEquivariant flow matching\nFor molecular sampling, flow matching trains continuous normalizing flows that transform samples from a simple distribution into molecular configurations. We will examine how rotations and permutations of atoms of the same species enter the model and training objective, discussing their effects on training and sampling efficiency. These flows can serve as Boltzmann generators, but their paths are not molecular dynamics trajectories.\nL. Klein, A. Krämer & F. Noé.\nEquivariant flow matching\n.\nAdvances in Neural Information Processing Systems\n36\n, 59886–59910 (2023).\nBackground: Y. Lipman, R. T. Q. Chen, H. Ben-Hamu, M. Nickel & M. Le.\nFlow Matching for Generative Modeling\n.\nInternational Conference on Learning Representations\n(2023).\nLonger time and length scales\n11\nLearning molecular dynamics\nFlashMD learns to predict atomic positions and momenta over time intervals longer than conventional molecular dynamics steps. We will compare this approach with Timewarp, which uses learned proposals and a Metropolis–Hastings correction to sample equilibrium configurations. The discussion will address the distinction between reproducing dynamical properties and equilibrium distributions, and the advantages and limitations of each approach.\nF. Bigi, S. Chong, A. Kristiadi & M. Ceriotti.\nFlashMD: long-stride, universal prediction of molecular dynamics\n.\nAdvances in Neural Information Processing Systems\n38\n, 98379–98416 (2025).\nL. Klein, A. Y. K. Foong, T. E. Fjelde, B. Mlodozeniec, M. Brockschmidt, S. Nowozin, F. Noé & R. Tomioka.\nTimewarp: Transferable Acceleration of Molecular Dynamics by Learning Time-Coarsened Dynamics\n.\nAdvances in Neural Information Processing Systems\n36\n, 52863–52883 (2023).\n12\nCoarse-grained potentials\nCoarse-grained models describe molecular systems with fewer degrees of freedom. We will discuss how force matching learns effective interactions from atomistic simulations, using CGnets and a transferable protein model as examples. The discussion will address the choice of representation, transfer to new proteins, and why reproducing equilibrium distributions does not guarantee correct dynamics.\nJ. Wang, S. Olsson, C. Wehmeyer, A. Pérez, N. E. Charron, G. de Fabritiis, F. Noé & C. Clementi.\nMachine Learning of Coarse-Grained Molecular Dynamics Force Fields\n.\nACS Central Science\n5\n, 755–767 (2019).\nFurther reading: N. E. Charron et al.\nNavigating protein landscapes with a machine-learned transferable coarse-grained model\n.\nNature Chemistry\n17\n, 1284–1292 (2025).\nFormat\nThe seminar begins with two introductory meetings on molecular simulations and statistical mechanics. Each participant then presents a selected research topic and prepares a written report discussing the methods, their strengths, limitations, and physical reliability. The report should incorporate feedback from the presentation and discussion and be submitted within four weeks after the presentation. It should be 5–8 pages in single-column format, including figures but excluding references. Participants read the assigned papers before each meeting and contribute to the discussion.\nSchedule\nWhen\nMeeting\n19 October 2026\n2:00–3:30 pm\nElectronic structure and molecular dynamics\nPotential-energy surfaces, DFT reference calculations, energies, and forces.\nSecond meeting\nStatistical mechanics and enhanced sampling\nBoltzmann distributions, free energy, rare events, and enhanced sampling.\nSubsequent meetings\nPresentations and discussion.\nThe remaining dates and rooms will be announced after the kickoff.\nLinks & contact\nRegistration\n·\nCourse catalogue\n·\nContact\nIf you cannot attend the kickoff, please email\nViktor Zaverkin\nwith your availability for the regular meetings.\nViktor Zaverkin · Machine Learning for Molecular and Materials Simulations\nWS 2026/27",
